1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,970
Imagine a company that has been
building software for twenty years.

2
00:00:04,840 --> 00:00:07,390
Its programmers know
what technical debt is.

3
00:00:08,157 --> 00:00:10,507
They know what outdated technology means.

4
00:00:11,217 --> 00:00:13,367
They know that support eventually ends.

5
00:00:14,177 --> 00:00:18,827
They know that one day the system
will have to be upgraded. And yet...

6
00:00:19,309 --> 00:00:23,099
...every so often, someone still has
to explain why we should upgrade.

7
00:00:23,969 --> 00:00:25,169
Welcome to IT.

8
00:00:26,085 --> 00:00:30,695
The most popular argument is:
"But it works." And yes. It does.

9
00:00:31,537 --> 00:00:33,167
Windows XP worked.

10
00:00:33,297 --> 00:00:36,547
A phone from 2008 can still work.

11
00:00:37,297 --> 00:00:39,667
A twenty-year-old car can still work.

12
00:00:40,384 --> 00:00:42,344
That doesn't necessarily
mean you should build

13
00:00:42,424 --> 00:00:45,054
the next ten years of
your business around it.

14
00:00:45,685 --> 00:00:48,255
Because "it works" is not a
property of your software.

15
00:00:48,745 --> 00:00:53,275
It's a property of your software
and everything around it. Today.

16
00:00:55,875 --> 00:00:57,985
Software doesn't exist in isolation.

17
00:00:59,195 --> 00:01:02,105
Every application depends
on languages, frameworks,

18
00:01:02,315 --> 00:01:05,745
libraries, operating systems,
databases and servers.

19
00:01:06,775 --> 00:01:08,645
And all of those things keep changing.

20
00:01:09,415 --> 00:01:10,905
Here's a real example.

21
00:01:12,015 --> 00:01:16,105
Imagine a device that uses a 2G
mobile network. It works perfectly.

22
00:01:17,115 --> 00:01:20,325
Then the operator announces:
"2G is going away."

23
00:01:21,121 --> 00:01:24,321
And you say: "But my
device still works!" Yes.

24
00:01:24,401 --> 00:01:26,331
But it needs the world around it.

25
00:01:26,841 --> 00:01:28,251
And the world has moved on.

26
00:01:30,631 --> 00:01:32,461
Now let's make this ridiculous.

27
00:01:33,691 --> 00:01:36,151
Imagine your water company
announces: "Starting

28
00:01:36,231 --> 00:01:38,201
next year, we're switching to Water 2.0."

29
00:01:39,838 --> 00:01:42,178
Your pipes still work.
Your sink still works.

30
00:01:42,258 --> 00:01:44,688
Your toilet still works.
Everything is fine.

31
00:01:45,762 --> 00:01:48,362
Except your plumbing
only supports Water 1.0.

32
00:01:48,662 --> 00:01:52,492
So you have two choices.
Replace your plumbing...

33
00:01:52,602 --> 00:01:57,152
or pay extra to keep receiving
the old water. Forever.

34
00:01:58,071 --> 00:02:03,141
Sounds ridiculous. And yet that is roughly
what happens with legacy technology.

35
00:02:04,143 --> 00:02:06,233
Old versions stop being supported.

36
00:02:06,463 --> 00:02:09,053
New software stops working with them.

37
00:02:09,443 --> 00:02:13,473
And eventually you pay extra just
to keep something obsolete alive.

38
00:02:15,933 --> 00:02:17,843
Let's make it even more ridiculous.

39
00:02:18,873 --> 00:02:21,183
Imagine a company that
makes office chairs.

40
00:02:21,693 --> 00:02:23,063
They release Chair 8.

41
00:02:23,693 --> 00:02:25,583
Your company has Chair 6.

42
00:02:26,275 --> 00:02:31,285
It works perfectly. It has four legs.
It has a backrest. Everything is fine.

43
00:02:32,090 --> 00:02:36,040
Then the manufacturer announces:
"Chair 6 is now out of support."

44
00:02:36,490 --> 00:02:39,780
No more replacement parts.
No more safety fixes.

45
00:02:40,700 --> 00:02:44,880
You say: "Why should we buy new
chairs? These ones work!" Fair enough.

46
00:02:44,960 --> 00:02:46,550
You can keep using Chair 6.

47
00:02:47,326 --> 00:02:50,376
But eventually you discover
that the new desks don't fit.

48
00:02:51,346 --> 00:02:53,356
The replacement legs aren't available.

49
00:02:54,226 --> 00:02:58,456
And the only person who knew how to
repair Chair 6 retired five years ago.

50
00:02:59,138 --> 00:03:01,048
Fortunately, there is a solution.

51
00:03:01,858 --> 00:03:03,988
Enterprise Extended Chair Support.

52
00:03:04,911 --> 00:03:07,591
For several million a
year, the manufacturer will

53
00:03:07,671 --> 00:03:10,661
keep making replacement legs
for your obsolete chairs.

54
00:03:11,535 --> 00:03:14,705
Completely absurd. But replace
"chair" with "software"...

55
00:03:15,155 --> 00:03:17,045
and suddenly nobody finds it strange.

56
00:03:19,775 --> 00:03:21,685
Except this part is not a joke.

57
00:03:22,595 --> 00:03:28,765
When Windows XP ran out of support in
2014, Microsoft sold exactly that deal.

58
00:03:29,473 --> 00:03:34,223
Custom support. Around two hundred
dollars per machine for the first year.

59
00:03:34,913 --> 00:03:36,383
Double that for the second.

60
00:03:37,210 --> 00:03:39,340
The British public sector signed it.

61
00:03:40,150 --> 00:03:43,880
Five and a half million pounds for
one more year of security updates.

62
00:03:44,881 --> 00:03:47,291
A year later they decided not to renew.

63
00:03:48,041 --> 00:03:49,971
And the old machines were still there.

64
00:03:50,675 --> 00:03:53,165
That is Enterprise Extended Chair Support.

65
00:03:54,015 --> 00:03:56,745
It just stops being funny
when the invoice is real.

66
00:03:59,725 --> 00:04:03,875
This is also why the question: "What
will the upgrade actually give us?"

67
00:04:04,365 --> 00:04:10,531
can be misleading. A new feature? Users
see it. A new button? Users see it.

68
00:04:10,611 --> 00:04:12,762
A technology upgrade? Nothing.

69
00:04:12,842 --> 00:04:14,832
And that is exactly the point.

70
00:04:15,717 --> 00:04:19,987
A successful upgrade has one very
boring result: Nothing happens.

71
00:04:20,897 --> 00:04:24,467
The application keeps working,
only now on supported technology.

72
00:04:25,500 --> 00:04:27,890
Although that is not
quite the whole truth.

73
00:04:28,600 --> 00:04:30,970
Old technology rarely fails by crashing.

74
00:04:31,891 --> 00:04:35,221
It fails when the security
audit comes back with a finding.

75
00:04:36,211 --> 00:04:39,781
When a partner's new interface needs
something your platform cannot speak.

76
00:04:40,587 --> 00:04:43,687
When the hosting provider
retires the image you run on.

77
00:04:43,767 --> 00:04:47,617
Or when you try to hire someone
and nobody wants the job.

78
00:04:48,322 --> 00:04:49,772
None of that is a bug.

79
00:04:50,322 --> 00:04:52,792
There is nothing to fix,
because nothing is broken.

80
00:04:53,742 --> 00:04:55,972
The world simply stopped waiting for you.

81
00:04:58,842 --> 00:05:00,932
It's like changing the oil in your car.

82
00:05:01,782 --> 00:05:03,992
Nobody gets excited about new oil.

83
00:05:04,242 --> 00:05:06,572
But skip the maintenance
for long enough...

84
00:05:07,302 --> 00:05:09,992
and eventually you get a
much more exciting problem.

85
00:05:10,727 --> 00:05:12,877
Technical debt works the same way.

86
00:05:13,567 --> 00:05:16,677
A small upgrade today might
mean: Update a few dependencies.

87
00:05:17,587 --> 00:05:20,917
Fix a few incompatibilities.
Run the tests. Done.

88
00:05:21,707 --> 00:05:25,487
Wait five years. Now it
is: Update the platform.

89
00:05:25,567 --> 00:05:28,737
Replace half the libraries.
Upgrade the framework.

90
00:05:29,687 --> 00:05:30,867
Rewrite that ancient component.

91
00:05:30,947 --> 00:05:32,417
And whatever you do...

92
00:05:32,767 --> 00:05:34,157
don't touch that file.

93
00:05:34,916 --> 00:05:37,326
At that point we are no
longer doing an upgrade.

94
00:05:38,296 --> 00:05:39,766
We are doing archaeology.

95
00:05:40,603 --> 00:05:46,073
"Look! I found a dependency from
2014!" "Don't touch it." "Why?"

96
00:05:46,503 --> 00:05:47,953
"We don't know what it does."

97
00:05:48,725 --> 00:05:52,955
Because technical debt isn't only
old software. It's also fear.

98
00:05:53,545 --> 00:05:56,355
Fear of changing something nobody
fully understands any more.

99
00:05:59,161 --> 00:06:01,551
And this is where an old saying comes in.

100
00:06:02,201 --> 00:06:04,131
The blacksmith's horse goes barefoot.

101
00:06:04,934 --> 00:06:08,624
A blacksmith spends his life making
shoes for everyone else's horses...

102
00:06:08,854 --> 00:06:11,024
while his own horse has none.

103
00:06:11,801 --> 00:06:13,621
English usually tells
it about the cobbler's

104
00:06:13,701 --> 00:06:19,391
children, who have nothing on their
feet. Different trade. Same blind spot.

105
00:06:20,285 --> 00:06:22,955
Software companies are
remarkably good at this.

106
00:06:23,405 --> 00:06:25,525
We spend decades explaining to customers

107
00:06:25,605 --> 00:06:28,335
why their old systems
need to be upgraded...

108
00:06:28,885 --> 00:06:31,515
while our own systems keep getting older.

109
00:06:32,232 --> 00:06:34,382
Because: "We don't have time right now."

110
00:06:34,932 --> 00:06:36,942
Next year: "Still not a priority."

111
00:06:38,090 --> 00:06:41,360
A few years later: "That's going
to be a pretty big upgrade."

112
00:06:42,170 --> 00:06:44,420
And eventually: "It's too big to upgrade."

113
00:06:47,290 --> 00:06:51,070
That is the trap. The longer
you postpone, the further your

114
00:06:51,150 --> 00:06:53,530
system drifts from the
technology around it.

115
00:06:53,610 --> 00:06:57,840
And closing that distance gets
more expensive every year.

116
00:06:58,505 --> 00:07:00,095
And none of this is a misunderstanding.

117
00:07:00,645 --> 00:07:02,415
They know it is old.

118
00:07:02,605 --> 00:07:04,555
They know support is ending.

119
00:07:05,025 --> 00:07:07,775
They know the upgrade will
eventually be necessary.

120
00:07:08,425 --> 00:07:10,195
It is a business decision.

121
00:07:10,982 --> 00:07:13,312
The cost of upgrading is visible today.

122
00:07:14,162 --> 00:07:16,972
The cost of not upgrading
is somewhere in the future.

123
00:07:17,757 --> 00:07:20,747
And those two costs don't
land on the same person.

124
00:07:21,677 --> 00:07:24,377
The upgrade comes out of
this year's budget, approved

125
00:07:24,457 --> 00:07:27,107
by someone who gets
nothing visible in return.

126
00:07:28,053 --> 00:07:31,223
The outage comes out of a
budget that doesn't exist yet,

127
00:07:31,433 --> 00:07:34,383
in a year when somebody else
will be sitting in that chair.

128
00:07:35,210 --> 00:07:40,240
Humans are remarkably good at sending
problems into the future. Unfortunately...

129
00:07:40,970 --> 00:07:42,760
the future eventually arrives.

130
00:07:43,722 --> 00:07:47,952
Technical debt doesn't disappear
when we ignore it. It grows.

131
00:07:50,552 --> 00:07:53,022
To be fair, not everything
needs upgrading.

132
00:07:53,612 --> 00:07:57,922
A system that gets switched off
next year can stay exactly as it is.

133
00:07:58,650 --> 00:08:02,240
And "there is a newer version"
is not an argument on its own.

134
00:08:02,949 --> 00:08:05,259
The question was never
"is this the latest?"

135
00:08:06,069 --> 00:08:10,319
The question is: "when this breaks,
will anyone still be able to fix it?"

136
00:08:10,961 --> 00:08:14,411
Which is why the healthy version of
this looks disappointingly boring.

137
00:08:15,381 --> 00:08:17,371
A few dependencies every release.

138
00:08:18,341 --> 00:08:20,271
One framework version at a time.

139
00:08:20,960 --> 00:08:22,890
Not a project. Not a migration.

140
00:08:23,160 --> 00:08:27,870
Not a two-year programme with a
steering committee. Just maintenance.

141
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:31,970
The same way nobody runs a
project to change the oil.

142
00:08:34,380 --> 00:08:38,530
So the next time someone asks: "Why
should we upgrade? It still works."

143
00:08:39,447 --> 00:08:43,087
Remember 2G. Remember Water 2.0.

144
00:08:43,627 --> 00:08:45,037
And remember Chair 6.

145
00:08:45,776 --> 00:08:49,706
Because "working today" is not the
same as "still working in five years".

146
00:08:50,256 --> 00:08:51,966
Technology moves on.

147
00:08:52,658 --> 00:08:56,428
And if we don't move with it, eventually
we are not maintaining our software.

148
00:08:57,298 --> 00:08:58,648
We are maintaining a museum.

149
00:09:01,568 --> 00:09:04,348
Which is exactly what the
blacksmith's horse was doing.

150
00:09:04,428 --> 00:09:06,618
Walking around barefoot...

151
00:09:06,848 --> 00:09:10,118
while the blacksmith was busy
making shoes for everyone else.
